# ~/dsgb10/pico.env on rank 0: the settings pico-engine.service, pico-front.service, pico-media.service and pico-ctl.sh read.
# systemd's EnvironmentFile syntax: KEY=value, no quotes, no inline comments, no shell expansion (a ~ stays a ~ and is
# not the home directory, so paths that are not the engine's own --weights use the full /home/<user>/... form; the
# engine expands a leading ~/ in --weights itself). A $VAR in a unit's ExecStart splits on spaces.
# <user> is the login that owns ~/dsgb10 on all four Sparks (the same name on each).

# ENGINE_ARGS: every rank gets this line. In order: the window (4M tokens), 32 lines, the memory left free on every Spark
# (--pool-free-gib), the Engram cache in the display reservation, copy drafting, the SSD tier size,
# batched drafting and its cost model, the 4-bit draft head and the Markov head, the confidence network, the certified head,
# direct ring on one switch, pico-dgx-ds4's balanced reduce-scatter (DSGB10_RING_RSBAL=1) and prefill tuning (DSGB10_PF_TUNE=hc,small,down),
# the abliteration file, each rank's quarter of the image encoder, the cache and caps of the
# front-end and the media worker, each rank's weight file, the decode index scan split over the ranks, the split cache, and the prefill passes of 16,384 rows
# (--prefill-rows-max 16384: a second set of row buffers the store lends while it is free; 0 reads 8,192 rows at a pass only).
# DSGB10_RING_RSBAL and DSGB10_PF_TUNE are pico-dgx-ds4's code (pico-dgx-ds4), switched on here.
# Copy drafting uses the engine's built-in table; to use a fitted one add
# --setenv DSGB10_COPY_TABLE=/home/<user>/dsgb10/copy-T.txt (same file, same path, on every Spark: docs/batching.md).
# Leave out --ablit and --vision-weights to serve without them.
ENGINE_ARGS=--max-ctx 4194304 --slots 32 --big-slots 0 --pool-free-gib 6.3 --engram-vram on --setenv DSGB10_COPY=1 --disk-gib 512 --setenv DSGB10_BUDGET=1 --setenv DSGB10_DRAFT1=6 --setenv DSGB10_WIDE=64 --setenv DSGB10_WIDE_COST=24,2.2,57,1.1 --draft-head4 --draft-markov8 --setenv DSGB10_HEAD4_C1=1 --setenv DSGB10_CF_MLP=/home/<user>/dsgb10/bin/cfmlp_cml.bin --setenv DSGB10_CF_ALPHA=1.0 --setenv DSGB10_BATCH_CF=1 --setenv DSGB10_BATCH_TRIM=1 --setenv DSGB10_BATCH_TRIM_COST=19,2.9 --setenv DSGB10_CERT=1 --setenv DSGB10_RING_DIRECT=2 --ablit /home/<user>/dsgb10/ablit.bin --vision-weights /home/<user>/dsgb10/vision/vision.rank{rank}.dsw --engram-cache-mib 2032 --front-mib 840 --media-mib 512 --weights ~/dsgb10/weights/rank{rank}.dsw --pscan all --kv-split tiered --prefill-rows-max 16384 --setenv DSGB10_RING_RSBAL=1 --setenv DSGB10_PF_TUNE=hc,small,down

# Sessions a planned stop keeps on every Spark's SSD, in GiB (0: none). A start needs MemFree of at least MIN_AVAIL_GIB
# on every Spark after the page cache is dropped (a rank takes about 90 GiB). GPU_CLOCK is the limit in MHz set on all
# four Sparks at every start: 2400, 2200, 2000 or 1800 (never unlocked).
KEEP_GIB=64
MIN_AVAIL_GIB=100
GPU_CLOCK=2200

# The front-end: its port, the address of rank 0 on your LAN (besides 127.0.0.1), how long a request is held while the
# engine restarts (seconds), and its other arguments: the checkpoint's tokenizer, the prompt encoding, the served name,
# thinking on at effort 75, the response cap (1,048,576 tokens), sampling defaults, each Spark's node sampler (rail A address,
# port 9760) for NODE HEALTH, the media worker's address, the fan and clock tool, the front-end's memory cap (the same
# number as the engine's --front-mib). Add --listen ADDR:PORT for another address; never 0.0.0.0.
PORT=8000
LAN_ADDR=<rank0-lan-address>
HOLD_S=900
FRONT_ARGS=--tokenizer /home/<user>/dsgb10/model/tokenizer.json --encoding 1017 --model-name deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017 --thinking --effort 75 --max-tokens 1048576 --temperature 1.0 --top-p 0.95 --node-stats <rank0-rail-a-address>:9760,<rank1-rail-a-address>:9760,<rank2-rail-a-address>:9760,<rank3-rail-a-address>:9760 --media-addr 127.0.0.1:18951 --fan-tool /usr/local/bin/dgx-fan-fleet --front-mib 840

# The checkpoint's folder (pico-media.service mounts its inference/config.json, read only) and the media worker's image tag
# (docker build -f Dockerfile.media-worker -t pico-media:rev1 in tools/vision).
MODEL_SNAPSHOT=/home/<user>/dsgb10/model
MEDIA_IMAGE=pico-media:rev1

# The clock tool (ds41f-4xgx10-switchless/tools/gpu-clock, installed by pico-ctl.sh install into ~/dsgb10/bin): run as
# "gpu-clock LIMIT [ssh targets...]" it sets the GPU clock limit (2400, 2200, 2000 or 1800 MHz; never unlocked) on this Spark
# and each target, and records {"limit","at","ok","error"} in GPU_CLOCK_STATE on the Spark it runs on (the targets are set,
# not recorded). pico-ctl.sh runs it on rank 0 at every start (GPU_CLOCK_TOOL); the dashboard's COOLING AND CLOCK box runs it
# through dgx-fan-fleet (DGX_FAN_CLOCK_TOOL). Both state settings name the same file.
# Leave GPU_CLOCK_TOOL out to have pico-ctl.sh call "sudo -n nvidia-smi -lgc 0,LIMIT" itself.
GPU_CLOCK_TOOL=/home/<user>/dsgb10/bin/gpu-clock
GPU_CLOCK_STATE=/home/<user>/dsgb10/run/gpu-clock-state

# The fan tool (dgx-fan-fleet, from the dgx-spark-fan-control project): the other three Sparks as ssh targets, the clock tool
# it runs for the dashboard's clock list, and the file where that tool records the limit (on the Spark it runs on). Leave these out, and
# --fan-tool in FRONT_ARGS, to have no COOLING AND CLOCK box.
DGX_FAN_NODES=<rank1-lan-address> <rank2-lan-address> <rank3-lan-address>
DGX_FAN_CLOCK_TOOL=/home/<user>/dsgb10/bin/gpu-clock
DGX_FAN_CLOCK_STATE=/home/<user>/dsgb10/run/gpu-clock-state
